<h2>Why exact dimensions matter more than they seem</h2>
<p>
  Passport photo requirements aren't just about overall photo size — they specify head size as a proportion of the frame (commonly a range like 50–69% of the photo's height, depending on the country), eye position within a defined vertical band, and centering tolerances. A photo cropped to the wrong overall dimensions almost always also gets the head-size proportion wrong, since that proportion is calculated against the specific frame size the office expects. This is why "close enough" so often isn't — a photo processed for the wrong country's dimensions, then just resized to the right pixel count, typically has an incorrect head-to-frame ratio even though the file dimensions now look correct.
</p>

<h2>Beyond size: what else gets checked</h2>
<p>
  Size is necessary but not sufficient for acceptance. Passport offices also evaluate:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Background</strong> — almost universally required to be plain white or light gray, with no shadows, patterns, or other people visible.</li>
  <li><strong>Lighting</strong> — even, front-facing light with no harsh shadows across the face or cast onto the background.</li>
  <li><strong>Expression</strong> — neutral expression, mouth closed, both eyes open and clearly visible (most countries prohibit smiling for passport photos specifically, unlike casual portraits).</li>
  <li><strong>Recency</strong> — most passport offices require a photo taken within the last 6 months, to ensure it reflects current appearance.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Getting the size and framing right handles the geometric part of the requirement; the rest depends on the actual photo shoot conditions.
</p>

